Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: honor the RX buffer allocation flags

stmmac_setup_dma_desc() passes GFP_KERNEL while bringing the interface
up, but stmmac_init_rx_buffers() discards it and uses GFP_ATOMIC. On the
256 MiB SG2002 this fails with ENOMEM while over 90 MiB is reclaimable
page cache. Reclaiming that cache manually makes the same open succeed.

Honor the caller's flags, retaining the existing GFP_DMA32 constraint.
The receive refill path continues to use its own atomic allocation.

This is the allocation-flags hunk from Ding Hui's posted v4 resume fix,
without the unrelated suspend/resume changes:
https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/20260627122533.1165324-1-dinghui1111@163.com/

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
@@ -1643,7 +1643,7 @@ static int stmmac_init_rx_buffers(struct stmmac_priv *priv,
 {
 	struct stmmac_rx_queue *rx_q = &dma_conf->rx_queue[queue];
 	struct stmmac_rx_buffer *buf = &rx_q->buf_pool[i];
-	gfp_t gfp = (GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOWARN);
+	gfp_t gfp = flags;
 
 	if (priv->dma_cap.host_dma_width <= 32)
 		gfp |= GFP_DMA32;
